                            <handNote xml:id="hand-1.1.1" source="#" medium="">
                                <locus from="" to=""/>
                                <persName ref="" role="scribe">[...] ben Menasseh, the physician</persName>
                                
                                
                                <placeName ref="https://jalit.org/place/13">Baṣra</placeName>
                                <origDate when="1020">1020</origDate>
                                <metamark function="vocalization">Sporadic use of Tiberian and Arabic vocalization. </metamark>
                                <metamark function="diacritics">Mostly unpointed. A dot above a letter is used to mark abbreviations as well as ellisions.</metamark>
                                <metamark function="divine-names">"Allah" is written with an Aleph-Lamed ligature followed by a horizontal stroke with a dot above and below.</metamark>
                                <note xml:lang="">
                                    <p>A circle indicates the place of corrections written in the margin possibly by a secondary hand.</p>
                                    <p>The writing is similar to the one in the related manuscripts, but more investigation is needed to clarify if it was copied by the same scribe. Note that eulogies and divine names are written differently compared to the other manuscripts and the scribe uses a dot instead of a stroke to mark abbreviations.</p>
                                    <p>Colophon included in Yevr.-Arab. II 3020.</p>
                                </note>
                            </handNote>
